            'body_en' => "In 1951 a young American archaeologist, Ralph Solecki, cut a trial trench into the floor of a cave the villagers of Shanidar used as a winter shelter for themselves and their animals. He came back in 1953, and again, and by 1960 his team had taken the floor down through fourteen metres of deposits — four great layers, from the Neolithic at the top to the Middle Palaeolithic at the bottom — and found the remains of ten Neanderthals: seven adults and two infants from the deep layers, then a tenth.\n\nThey were not what the textbooks expected. One man had lived for years with a crushed eye socket, a withered arm and a damaged leg: others had fed him. Another had been laid in the ground beside clusters of flower pollen. For the first time, Neanderthals looked like people who cared for each other and buried their dead with intent. Later work has argued over the flowers — a burrowing rodent may have brought them — but not over the care, and not over the burials.\n\nThe cave is still being read. A Cambridge-led team returned in 2014; in 2018 it found a new, crushed skeleton beside Solecki's old trench, and in 2020 published it as Shanidar Z. In the layers above the Neanderthals, people of the Zarzian culture used the cave about 10,600 BC and buried some thirty-five of their dead on the slope: among the first cemeteries of the first villagers of the Zagros.",
